The Scientific Tension Engine

A Control Architecture for Autonomous Scientific Discovery via Persistent Contradiction, Context, and Experimental Resolution

Theoretical PhD Proposal

Author / Research Coordinator: Ouadi Maakoul
Research Program: For Humanity 4

---

Abstract

Artificial intelligence systems are increasingly capable of generating hypotheses, retrieving literature, constructing knowledge graphs, designing experiments, and producing scientific manuscripts. The AI Scientist, AI co-scientist, and SciAgents have demonstrated that autonomous scientific workflows are technically plausible. However, these systems organize discovery around hypothesis generation, task objectives, novelty, plausibility, or experimental optimization.

This work proposes a complementary paradigm: Scientific Tension-Driven Discovery (STDD). The central hypothesis is that an autonomous scientific system should maintain, in addition to representations of established knowledge and unresolved gaps, a persistent representation of tensions between scientific claims, models, observations, assumptions, mechanisms, and boundary conditions. These tensions function as an explicit control state determining which investigation the system pursues next.

The proposed system, the Scientific Tension Engine (STE), represents scientific knowledge as provenance-aware, context-indexed claims with uncertainty, assumptions, and evidence. It detects structured tensions via a revision-cost functional grounded in AGM belief revision, stores them in a persistent Tension Memory modeled as an ATMS nogood store, prioritizes them via expected value of information, and converts high-value tensions into discriminating experiments, counterexamples, or theoretical analyses.

The proposed discovery cycle is:

\mathcal{K}_t \;\rightarrow\; \mathcal{T}_t \;\rightarrow\; Q_t \;\rightarrow\; E_t \;\rightarrow\; O_{t+1} \;\rightarrow\; \mathcal{K}_{t+1}

where \mathcal{K} is the knowledge state, \mathcal{T} the tension state, Q a scientific question or counterexample, E an investigation, and O the resulting observation.

The core research question is not merely whether AI can generate scientific hypotheses, but whether it can systematically identify, prioritize, and resolve high-value tensions within heterogeneous scientific knowledge, thereby discovering mechanisms or boundary conditions that hypothesis-generation-centered workflows would leave hidden.

This proposal claims neither contradiction detection nor belief revision nor active learning nor falsification as novel. The proposed contribution is the integration of persistent, provenance-aware tension into an autonomous scientific control architecture, together with a formal representation, a single-domain benchmark (TensionBench v0), and evaluation metrics for measuring tension-to-discovery conversion. The central hypothesis is stated falsifiably, and the proposal specifies the conditions under which it must be rejected.

