2. Related Work and Novelty Boundary

2.1 Autonomous scientific discovery

The AI Scientist (Lu et al., 2024) demonstrated end-to-end automated workflows spanning idea generation, code, experiments, visualization, manuscript production, and automated review. AI co-scientist (Gottweis et al., 2025) introduced a multi-agent architecture for hypothesis generation and validation. SciAgents (Ghafarollahi & Buehler, 2025) combined large scientific knowledge graphs with LLMs and multi-agent reasoning. Earlier work on the Robot Scientist (King et al., 2009) demonstrated closed-loop hypothesis generation and experimental testing in yeast genetics. Langley et al. (1987) established computational models of scientific discovery as a research program.

These systems establish that autonomous workflows are technically plausible. The STE therefore does not ask whether AI can perform scientific research. It asks: What should determine the next scientific investigation?

2.2 Contradiction detection and scientific claim verification

Contradiction detection has precedent in natural language inference, scientific claim verification (SciFact: Wadden et al., 2020; FEVER: Thorne et al., 2018), knowledge-graph inconsistency handling, and biomedical literature mining (Alamri & Stevenson, 2016). The SciClaim corpus (Magnusson & Friedman, 2021) provides fine-grained qualifier labels including hedging, monotonicity, and certainty.

The STE builds on these traditions without claiming them as novel. Its contribution is not the detection of contradictory claim pairs, but the representation of tensions as persistent control objects with provenance, context, and revision cost.

2.3 Belief revision and truth maintenance

Belief revision is formalized by AGM (Alchourrón, Gärdenfors & Makinson, 1985) and developed in Gärdenfors (1988) and Hansson (1999). Truth maintenance systems (de Kleer, 1986) provide the machinery for tracking dependencies and nogoods in non-monotonic reasoning. The STE adopts both traditions: AGM for the revision-cost functional, ATMS for the persistent memory of tensions.

2.4 Argumentation, inconsistency measures, and paraconsistency

Argumentation frameworks (Dung, 1995) provide semantics for conflicting arguments. Inconsistency measures (Hunter & Konieczny, 2010) quantify the severity of conflicts in belief sets. Paraconsistent logics (Priest, 1979) permit reasoning in the presence of contradiction without explosion. The STE does not adopt a paraconsistent logic as its base, but it is designed to operate in the presence of unresolved inconsistency and to treat that inconsistency as a first-class control signal rather than a failure state.

2.5 Literature-based discovery

Literature-based discovery is formalized by Swanson (1986, 1988) through the ABC model: given two non-interacting literatures L_1 linking A to B and L_2 linking B to C, a hypothesis A \to C may be discoverable by bridging. Swanson's classic examples are Raynaud's disease + fish oil (1986) and migraine + magnesium (1988). The STE uses the same logic for retrospective evaluation.

2.6 Active learning and Bayesian experimental design

Active learning (Settles, 2009) and Bayesian optimal experimental design (Chaloner & Verdinelli, 1995) select experiments by expected information gain. The expected value of sample information (Lindley, 1956) provides the underlying utility functional. The STE extends this logic by making tension resolution an explicit term in the acquisition objective.

2.7 Philosophy of science

The framework is informed by Popper's (1959) falsifiability criterion, Lakatos's (1970) methodology of scientific research programmes, Kuhn's (1962) account of paradigm shifts, and Thagard's (1992) computational theory of conceptual revolutions. The STE does not claim to resolve philosophical debates about scientific progress; it operationalizes one specific signal — unresolved tension — as a control variable for autonomous investigation.

2.8 Novelty hypothesis

The following components are not claimed as individually novel: scientific hypothesis generation; LLM scientific agents; knowledge graphs; contradiction detection; claim verification; active learning; automated experimental design; falsification; adversarial evaluation; persistent research memory; inconsistency measurement; truth maintenance.

The proposed novelty hypothesis is:

A general-purpose autonomous scientific architecture in which persistent, provenance-aware tensions form an explicit computational control state that governs question generation, counterexample construction, experiment selection, and knowledge revision.

This is stated as a hypothesis, not as an established contribution. A systematic review (Phase I) is required before it may be asserted.

