3.2 Knowledge state and control memories

The scientific knowledge state at time t is

\mathcal{K}_t = (C_t,\; \mathcal{M}_t,\; \mathrm{Prov}_t),

where:

· C_t — set of claims (§3.3);
· \mathcal{M}_t — set of models or mechanisms;
· \mathrm{Prov}_t — provenance and evidence relations.

Two auxiliary memories are maintained persistently and are not part of the asserted knowledge state:

M_G = \text{Gap Memory}, \qquad M_T = \text{Tension Memory}.

This separation is deliberate. The knowledge state \mathcal{K}_t represents what the system currently asserts. The Gap Memory M_G records what the system knows it does not know. The Tension Memory M_T records what the system knows does not fit together. The discovery controller consults all three but treats them as distinct control inputs:

\mathcal{C}\bigl(\mathcal{K}_t,\; M_G,\; M_T,\; \omega_t,\; A_t,\; \kappa_t\bigr) \;\rightarrow\; Q_t,

where:

· \omega_t — objective state (goals, scientific aims);
· A_t — available action space (experiments, simulations, retrieval);
· \kappa_t — cost budget (computational, temporal, financial).

3.3 Claim representation

A scientific claim is represented as a structured, context-indexed assertion:

c_i = (s_i,\; p_i,\; o_i,\; \Gamma_i,\; A_i,\; U_i,\; P_i,\; E_i),

where:

· s_i — subject;
· p_i — predicate;
· o_i — object;
· \Gamma_i — context (population, regime, instrument, conditions);
· A_i — assumptions;
· U_i — uncertainty representation (credal set with a distinguished reference element);
· P_i — provenance (source, method, reproducibility);
· E_i — evidence (supporting data, statistics).

Claims are not bare triples: they are context-indexed, uncertainty-weighted, provenance-aware assertions. A claim without context is under-specified; a claim without uncertainty is over-confident; a claim without provenance is un-auditable.

3.4 Belief revision preliminaries (AGM)

Let \mathcal{L} be a propositional language and K a belief set (a deductively closed set of sentences in \mathcal{L}). Following AGM:

· Expansion: K + \alpha = \mathrm{Cn}(K \cup \{\alpha\});
· Contraction: K - \alpha removes \alpha while preserving deductive closure;
· Revision (Levi identity): K * \alpha = (K - \neg \alpha) + \alpha.

Contraction is characterized by an epistemic entrenchment ordering \leq_E over sentences, or equivalently by a partial-meet selection over the remainder set

K \perp \alpha \;=\; \{\, X \subseteq K \;:\; X \not\vdash \alpha,\; X \subsetneq Y \subseteq K \Rightarrow Y \vdash \alpha \,\}.

A partial-meet contraction is

K - \alpha \;=\; \bigcap \gamma(K \perp \alpha),

where \gamma is a selection function returning a nonempty subset of K \perp \alpha when the latter is nonempty.

For our purposes, the relevant belief set is the union of context, assumptions, and uncertainty constraints of two claims:

\mathcal{B}_{ij} \;=\; \mathrm{Cn}\bigl(\Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j \cup A_i \cup A_j \cup U_i \cup U_j\bigr).

Explicit idealization. AGM assumes deductive closure and logical omniscience. Scientific knowledge is open, probabilistic, and often paraconsistent. The STE adopts AGM as a normative idealization for the revision-cost functional, not as a descriptive model of scientific practice. Extensions to non-prioritized revision, paraconsistent base logics, and resource-bounded reasoning are left to future work (§14).

3.5 Operational definition of tension

A naive detector evaluates c_i \perp c_j directly. The STE instead evaluates the minimal revision cost required to render the two claims jointly consistent.

Definition 3.5.1 (RevisionCost). Let \Gamma_i, \Gamma_j be contexts, A_i, A_j assumption sets, U_i, U_j uncertainty representations, and P_{ij} = P_i \cup P_j the joint provenance. Define

$$
\rho(c_i, c_j) \;=\; \min_{(\Gamma^, A^, U^)} \Bigl[
\lambda_1 , d_\Gamma(\Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j,, \Gamma^)

· \lambda_2 , d_A(A_i \cup A_j,, A^*)
· \lambda_3 , D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U^* ,\|, U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}})
· \lambda_4 , \bigl(1 - q(P_{ij})\bigr)
  \Bigr],
  $$

where:

· d_\Gamma, d_A are symmetric differences (Jaccard or cardinality-based) over context and assumption sets;
· U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} is a reference joint uncertainty (§3.6);
· q(P_{ij}) \in [0,1] is the provenance quality of the joint provenance, defined below;
· \lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_4 > 0 are domain-calibrated weights.

The minimization ranges over (\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*) such that the two claims are jointly consistent under the revised constraints, i.e., \Gamma^* \cup A^* \cup U^* \not\vdash \bot with c_i, c_j both admissible.

Provenance quality. The penalty term is 1 - q(P_{ij}), not |P_{ij}|. A larger provenance set may indicate stronger evidence, not weaker. Define

q(P) \;=\; \sum_{j} w_j \, q_j(P), \qquad \sum_j w_j = 1, \quad q_j \in [0,1],

where the q_j capture source reliability, methodological rigor (sample size, preregistration, design), reproducibility (independent replication), and recency. For a joint provenance P_{ij} = P_i \cup P_j, define

q(P_{ij}) \;=\; \min\bigl(q(P_i),\, q(P_j)\bigr),

a conservative choice: the joint claim is only as reliable as its weakest source.

Definition 3.5.2 (Tension score). Given a domain threshold \theta > 0 and a scale \rho_0 > 0, define

\tau_{ij} \;=\; 1 - \exp\!\left(-\frac{\rho(c_i, c_j)}{\rho_0}\right) \in [0, 1),

so that \tau_{ij} \to 0 as claims become jointly consistent without revision, and \tau_{ij} \to 1 as revision cost becomes prohibitive.

A scientific tension is said to obtain when \tau_{ij} \geq \theta.

Definition 3.5.3 (Actionable tension). A tension \mathfrak{t} is actionable if and only if:

1. \tau_{ij} \geq \theta (detected with sufficient score);
2. q(P_{ij}) \geq q_{\min} (survives provenance audit);
3. there exists E \in A_t with \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) > 0 (admits a non-trivial investigation).

Only actionable tensions enter the prioritization queue.

3.6 Well-definedness and computational remarks

Reference joint uncertainty. Let each U_i be a credal set (convex set of probability distributions) over the quantity of interest, with a distinguished reference element p_i \in U_i (e.g., the maximum-entropy element). Define

U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} \;=\; \text{maximum-entropy coupling of } p_i, p_j,

under a specified dependence structure (independence by default, copula-based otherwise). This coupling always exists and is a valid probability distribution, so D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U^* \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}}) is well-defined for any U^* on the same space.

Consistency set. Define

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} \;=\; \{\, U \;:\; c_i, c_j \text{ admissible under } \Gamma^*, A^*, U \,\}.

If \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} = \varnothing, no uncertainty revision can dissolve the tension, and \rho = \infty (capped at a large finite value). If \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} \neq \varnothing and is convex, the KL projection

U^* \;=\; \arg\min_{U \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}}} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U \,\|\, U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}})

exists and is unique.

Closed forms.

· Boolean context and assumptions. If \Gamma, A are finite sets and U is degenerate, then d_\Gamma, d_A are symmetric differences and \rho is exactly the minimum number of context or assumption literals that must be added or removed to remove the joint inconsistency. This is the AGM partial-meet contraction cost on a finite language.
· Gaussian uncertainty. If U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} is Gaussian \mathcal{N}(\mu, \Sigma) and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} is defined by linear constraints on the mean \mu, the KL projection (moment projection) admits a closed form: the projected distribution is Gaussian with the constrained mean and the same covariance (or adjusted covariance under joint constraints). If the constraints involve the covariance, numerical optimization is required.

Complexity. Computing \rho requires (i) determining whether \mathcal{B}_{ij} is consistent, which is a satisfiability problem over the joint constraint set, and (ii) finding the minimal-cost edit (\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*) restoring consistency. Step (i) is NP-complete for propositional languages; step (ii) is at least as hard as minimal unsatisfiable subset (MUS) extraction, which is computationally intractable in the worst case. Practical implementations therefore rely on MUS enumeration (MARCO: Liffiton et al., 2016; CAMUS: Liffiton & Sakallah, 2008) and heuristic search over (\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*).

3.7 Demarcation criteria

A persistent risk in any tension-based architecture is overbreadth: treating gaps, uncertainty, noise, and terminological differences as tensions. The following demarcation criteria are therefore adopted.

Concept Definition Distinct from tension because
Gap Absence of a claim Tension requires the presence of incompatible claims
Uncertainty Distribution over a single claim's value Tension requires incompatibility between claims
Noise Unexplained variance within a single claim's evidence Tension requires incompatibility across claims
Terminological difference Semantic misalignment between claims Dissolves under semantic alignment; tension does not
Tension Incompatibility between claims that resists minimal revision —

A tension is productive if it is actionable (§3.5.3) and survives provenance audit. A tension is an artifact if it dissolves under semantic alignment, provenance audit, or a small revision. The artifact-vs-genuine distinction is central to §9.

3.8 Taxonomy of scientific tension

Type Description Formal signature
T1 — Direct contradiction P(x) vs. \neg P(x) under identical context \Gamma_i = \Gamma_j, A_i = A_j, \rho large
T2 — Contextual tension P(x \mid \Gamma_1) \neq P(x \mid \Gamma_2) with \Gamma_1 \neq \Gamma_2 Small d_\Gamma revision dissolves
T3 — Mechanistic tension M_1 \to Y and M_2 \to Y both compatible with evidence Evidence does not discriminate
T4 — Boundary tension \mathcal{M}(x) \approx y for x \in \Omega_1, but \mathcal{M}(x) \not\approx y for x \in \Omega_2 Boundary becomes the object of discovery
T5 — Anomalous observation P(y \mid M) \ll \epsilon Model failure, missing variable, or new phenomenon
T6 — Cross-domain tension M_A \neq M_B despite overlapping observations Hidden variables, scale incompatibility, missing abstraction
T7 — Temporal tension \mathcal{K}_{t_1} \models c, \mathcal{K}_{t_2} \models \neg c Historical scientific state preserved rather than overwritten

The taxonomy is descriptive, not exhaustive. It provides annotation categories for TensionBench v0 (§4) and a vocabulary for status reporting.

3.9 Tension Memory as an ATMS nogood store

The central architectural component is the persistent Tension Memory

M_T = \{\mathfrak{t}_1, \mathfrak{t}_2, \ldots, \mathfrak{t}_n\},

where each record is

\mathfrak{t} = \bigl(C^+,\; C^-,\; \Gamma,\; A,\; E,\; P,\; U,\; S,\; H\bigr),

with:

· C^+ — supporting claims;
· C^- — conflicting claims;
· \Gamma — joint context;
· A — joint assumptions;
· E — evidence;
· P — provenance;
· U — uncertainty;
· S — status;
· H — historical resolution trajectory.

Statuses:

S \in \{\text{unverified},\; \text{contextualized},\; \text{resolved},\; \text{partially resolved},\; \text{rejected},\; \text{confirmed},\; \text{persistent}\}.

Formal connection to ATMS. In de Kleer's Assumption-Based Truth Maintenance System, a nogood is a minimal set of assumptions whose joint adoption is inconsistent. The label of a node is the set of minimal environments supporting it. The STE's M_T is an augmented ATMS nogood store:

· each \mathfrak{t} \in M_T corresponds to a minimal inconsistent environment
  e_{\mathfrak{t}} \;\subseteq\; \Gamma \cup A, \qquad e_{\mathfrak{t}} \cup \{\text{background}\} \vdash \bot,
  
  with no proper subset of e_{\mathfrak{t}} inconsistent;
· the status S tracks whether the nogood has been retired, contextualized (transformed into a boundary condition), or confirmed as persistent;
· the history H records the sequence of contractions and revisions attempted.

This identification is not cosmetic: it inherits ATMS's efficient label propagation, minimality guarantees, and its distinction between conflicts and support sets, which is exactly the distinction the STE needs between "genuine tension" and "contextual difference."

Minimality. The ATMS nogood is minimal if no proper subset is a nogood. Scientific tensions are not always minimal: a tension may involve redundant assumptions. The STE distinguishes minimal tensions (ATMS nogoods, used for efficient propagation) from reported tensions (possibly non-minimal, used for human interpretation). Minimality is enforced by MUS extraction when required; otherwise it is a heuristic.

Status transition function. Define

\delta_S : S \times \Omega \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \;\rightarrow\; S,

where \Omega is the outcome space:

\Omega = \{\text{revision\_small\_cost},\; \text{revision\_large\_cost},\; \text{experiment\_discriminates},\; \text{experiment\_ambiguous},\; \text{provenance\_audit\_pass},\; \text{provenance\_audit\_fail},\; \text{mechanism\_identified}\}.

Representative transitions:

Current S Outcome Next S
unverified revision_small_cost contextualized
unverified revision_large_cost persistent
unverified provenance_audit_fail rejected
unverified experiment_discriminates confirmed
contextualized mechanism_identified resolved
contextualized experiment_ambiguous persistent
confirmed mechanism_identified resolved
confirmed experiment_ambiguous persistent
persistent mechanism_identified resolved
persistent provenance_audit_fail rejected
partially_resolved mechanism_identified resolved
any provenance_audit_fail rejected

3.10 Control architecture

The discovery controller is

\mathcal{C}\bigl(\mathcal{K}_t,\; M_G,\; M_T,\; \omega_t,\; A_t,\; \kappa_t\bigr) \;\rightarrow\; Q_t,

with the following coupled subsystems:

1. Tension detector \mathcal{T}: \mathcal{K}_t \to \mathcal{T}_t, producing tensions via §3.5.
2. Memory update M_T \leftarrow M_T \cup \mathcal{T}_t, with status reconciliation via \delta_S.
3. Prioritizer \Pi: M_T \times A_t \times \kappa_t \to \mathbb{R} (§3.11).
4. Question generator Q: \mathfrak{t}^* \mapsto Q_t.
5. Experiment selector E^*: Q_t \mapsto E_t (§3.12).
6. Revision \mathcal{K}_{t+1} = \mathrm{Rev}(\mathcal{K}_t, O_{t+1}, \mathfrak{t}^*) (§3.13).

3.11 Prioritization via value of information

Not every contradiction is scientifically important. Following Lindley (1956) and Chaloner & Verdinelli (1995), we define the value of information of an intervention E targeting tension \mathfrak{t} as the expected reduction in posterior entropy over the hypothesis set \mathcal{H}:

\mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{y \sim P(\cdot \mid E, \mathfrak{t})} \Bigl[\, D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\bigl(P(\mathcal{H} \mid y, E, \mathfrak{t}) \,\big\|\, P(\mathcal{H} \mid \mathfrak{t})\bigr) \Bigr].

This is the expected information gain (EIG) of E with respect to \mathcal{H}. Priorities are

\Pi(\mathfrak{t}) \;=\; \max_{E \in A_t} \Bigl[\, \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) \;+\; \mu_F \, F(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) \;-\; \mu_C \, \mathrm{Cost}(E) \,\Bigr],

where:

· F(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) — falsification utility, defined as the probability that E produces an observation in the low-density region of the current best model:
  F(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) \;=\; P_{y \sim P(\cdot \mid E)}\bigl(y \in \mathcal{Y}_{\mathrm{anom}}(\mathcal{M})\bigr);
· \mathrm{Cost}(E) — computational, temporal, financial, or physical cost, expressed in explicit units (API calls, compute hours, retrieval budget, expert review time);
· \mu_F, \mu_C \geq 0 — trade-off parameters.

Note on additivity. \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) already subsumes model discrimination when \mathcal{H} is taken to be the set of competing mechanisms \{M_1, \ldots, M_n\}. The earlier notation D(E) for discrimination and \mathrm{IG}(E) for information gain therefore refer to the same functional; they must not be added as separate terms. The correct joint objective is \mathrm{VOI} + \mu_F F - \mu_C \mathrm{Cost}, as above. F is retained as an additive term because it measures a distinct target (probability of anomaly) rather than entropy reduction.

Linear surrogate. For computational tractability, the following proposition justifies a weighted-sum surrogate.

Proposition 3.11.1 (Linear VOI surrogate). Suppose:

1. P(\mathcal{H} \mid y, E, \mathfrak{t}) is locally Gaussian around a maximum;
2. the log-likelihood \log P(y \mid \mathcal{H}, E) is locally quadratic in \mathcal{H};
3. the components I, U, D, F defined below are conditionally independent given \mathfrak{t}.

Then \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) admits the first-order approximation

\mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) \;\approx\; w_I I(\mathfrak{t}) + w_U U(\mathfrak{t}) + w_D D(\mathfrak{t}) + w_F F(\mathfrak{t}),

with weights w_\bullet determined by the local curvature of the posterior and the entropy Hessian at the current MAP estimate.

Proof sketch. Under (1)–(3), the entropy of the posterior over \mathcal{H} is a quadratic form in the Fisher information contributed by E; its expected reduction is linear in the independent contributions I, U, D, F. The coefficients are the diagonal entries of the entropy Hessian. \square

Corollary. The weighted-sum form is admissible as a surrogate, but the weights are not free parameters: they must be calibrated to the local curvature, either by cross-validation on TensionBench or by direct Hessian estimation. Free weights with no calibration risk collinearity between D and F; the STE therefore uses the VOI form directly whenever the posterior is tractable, and the linear surrogate only under the stated assumptions.

The highest-priority tension is

\mathfrak{t}^* \;=\; \arg\max_{\mathfrak{t} \in M_T} \Pi(\mathfrak{t}).

3.12 Counterexample and experiment selection

Given a selected tension \mathfrak{t}^* with competing models M_1, \ldots, M_n, the STE searches for an intervention E maximizing the discrimination between them:

E^* \;=\; \arg\max_{E \in A_t} \Bigl[\, \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}^*) \;+\; \mu_F F(E \mid \mathfrak{t}^*) \;-\; \mu_C \mathrm{Cost}(E) \,\Bigr].

The counterexample generator produces candidate inputs

x^* \;=\; \arg\max_x \, D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\bigl(P(y \mid M_1(x)) \,\big\|\, P(y \mid M_2(x))\bigr)

or, in the falsification mode,

x^* \;=\; \arg\min_x \, P(y \mid M(x)).

This makes the abstract tension formally actionable: the system does not merely flag a contradiction, it constructs the smallest discriminating experiment.

3.13 Modular revision of the knowledge state

Given \mathcal{K}_t = (C_t, \mathcal{M}_t, \mathrm{Prov}_t) and an observation O_{t+1} arising from experiment E_t targeting tension \mathfrak{t}^*, the revision operator is defined componentwise:

\mathcal{K}_{t+1} \;=\; \mathrm{Rev}(\mathcal{K}_t, O_{t+1}, \mathfrak{t}^*) \;=\; \bigl(\mathrm{Rev}_C(C_t, O_{t+1}),\; \mathrm{Rev}_{\mathcal{M}}(\mathcal{M}_t, O_{t+1}),\; \mathrm{Rev}_{\mathrm{Prov}}(\mathrm{Prov}_t, O_{t+1})\bigr),

with:

1. Identify the conflicting sentence \alpha \in \mathrm{Cn}(C_t) implicated by \mathfrak{t}^*.
2. Contract: C_t^- = C_t - \alpha (AGM partial-meet contraction, §3.4).
3. Expand: C_{t+1} = C_t^- + \{\text{claims extracted from } O_{t+1}\}.
4. Update models: \mathcal{M}_{t+1} = \mathrm{Update}(\mathcal{M}_t, O_{t+1}).
5. Extend provenance: \mathrm{Prov}_{t+1} = \mathrm{Prov}_t \cup \mathrm{Prov}(O_{t+1}).
6. Update M_T: apply \delta_S to \mathfrak{t}^* and to any tension affected by the revision.
7. Update M_G: remove gaps resolved by O_{t+1}; add newly identified gaps.

This modular operator ensures that revision propagates correctly to claims, models, provenance, gaps, and tensions, rather than being confined to a single layer.

3.14 Worked example (propositional case)

Let \mathcal{L} be a propositional language with atoms \{x_{>0}, x_{<0}, \text{dose}_{10}, \text{linear}, P\}.

Claims.

· c_1: P, with \Gamma_1 = \{x_{>0}, \text{dose}_{10}\}, A_1 = \{\text{linear}\}, U_1 degenerate, P_1 = \{S_1\}, q(P_1) = 0.9.
· c_2: \neg P, with \Gamma_2 = \{x_{<0}, \text{dose}_{10}\}, A_2 = \{\text{linear}\}, U_2 degenerate, P_2 = \{S_2\}, q(P_2) = 0.85.

Joint belief set.

\mathcal{B}_{12} = \mathrm{Cn}\bigl(\{x_{>0}, x_{<0}, \text{dose}_{10}, \text{linear}, P, \neg P\}\bigr).

Since \{x_{>0}, x_{<0}\} is inconsistent (x cannot be both positive and negative) and \{P, \neg P\} is inconsistent, \mathcal{B}_{12} is inconsistent.

Revision options.

· Context revision alone: remove \{x_{>0}, x_{<0}\}, keep \{\text{dose}_{10}\}. Then c_1, c_2 directly contradict (P vs. \neg P); still inconsistent.
· Context and assumption revision: remove \{x_{>0}, x_{<0}\}, revise A^* = \{\text{linear}, \text{regime\_dependent}\}. Then c_1, c_2 can both hold under distinct regimes.

Computation. Take the second option. Then

d_\Gamma(\Gamma_1 \cup \Gamma_2, \Gamma^*) = |\{x_{>0}, x_{<0}\}| = 2,


d_A(A_1 \cup A_2, A^*) = |\{\text{regime\_dependent}\}| = 1,


D_{\mathrm{KL}} = 0 \quad \text{(degenerate uncertainty)},


q(P_{12}) = \min(0.9, 0.85) = 0.85.

With \lambda_1 = \lambda_2 = \lambda_4 = 1:

\rho(c_1, c_2) \;=\; 2 + 1 + 0 + 0.15 \;=\; 3.15.

With \rho_0 = 1:

\tau_{12} \;=\; 1 - e^{-3.15} \;\approx\; 0.957.

With threshold \theta = 0.5: the tension is detected. Its type is T4 (boundary tension) and T2 (contextual tension). The minimal revision requires introducing a regime variable — this is exactly the boundary condition the STE should discover.

The example demonstrates that:

1. The RevisionCost is computable exactly in the Boolean case.
2. Context revision alone may be insufficient; the joint (\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*) optimization is necessary.
3. The tension score saturates toward 1 as revision cost becomes prohibitive.

