4. TensionBench v0

TensionBench is not an accessory. It is the empirical foundation of the PhD and must be built before the full engine.

4.1 Domain scope

TensionBench v0 is built in a single domain: biomedical drug repurposing contradictions, using the literature on context-dependent drug effects (Sosa & Altman, 2022, provides annotated context-vs-contradiction examples).

Single-domain scope is deliberate: a multi-domain v0 would confound "is tension-prioritization useful?" with "can claims be extracted reliably across five different extraction problems?" The single-domain design isolates the variable of interest.

4.2 Seed corpora

· HealthContradict — 920 expert-verified instances of health-claim contradictions (npj Digital Medicine, 2026; DOI: 10.1038/s41746-025-02336-0).
· ManCon — 259 manually curated contradictory claims from 24 systematic reviews on cardiovascular topics (Alamri & Stevenson, 2016).
· SciFact (Wadden et al., 2020) — protocol reference: 1,409 claims over 5,183 abstracts, with atomic claims extracted from citation sentences and Supports/Refutes annotations with rationales.
· SciClaim (Magnusson & Friedman, 2021) — 12,738 fine-grained labels for claim qualifiers (hedging, monotonicity, certainty).

4.3 Annotation schema

Each TensionBench v0 item contains:

· atomic claim pairs (c_i, c_j) per §3.3;
· extracted context \Gamma_i, \Gamma_j;
· assumptions A_i, A_j;
· uncertainty U_i, U_j;
· provenance P_i, P_j with quality scores q(P_i), q(P_j);
· expert label: T1–T7 taxonomy plus artifact;
· historical resolution status: resolved, contextualized, persistent, rejected;
· where available, the historical experiment or analysis that resolved it.

4.4 Scale, pre-registration, and power analysis

The target is 600 annotated tension pairs. This is provisional. The following protocol is adopted:

1. Pre-registration. The annotation codebook, sampling strategy, success criteria, and analysis plan are frozen and registered before annotation begins.
2. Pilot. 50–100 pairs are annotated by two experts. Krippendorff's \alpha is computed. If \alpha < 0.67, the codebook is revised and the pilot repeated.
3. Power analysis. A power analysis is conducted to determine the minimum number of pairs required to detect the hypothesized effect sizes (H1: 15% relative reduction in false positives; H4: 20% relative improvement in Discovery Yield) at conventional significance levels (\alpha = 0.05, power = 0.80). The final sample size is set by this analysis and pre-registered.
4. Scale-up. Annotation proceeds at the pre-registered scale, with periodic \alpha monitoring.
5. Fallback. If annotation quality cannot be maintained at scale, the benchmark is reduced to 300 high-quality pairs. Annotation quality dominates quantity.

4.5 Protocol

· Claim extraction: SciFact-style atomic claims from citation sentences.
· Labeling: Supports / Refutes / Contextual / Ambiguous, with rationale.
· Qualifier tagging: SciClaim-style hedging and monotonicity labels.
· Inter-annotator agreement: Krippendorff's \alpha \geq 0.67 required before use.
· Artifact labeling: Each pair is labeled artifact or genuine tension by human experts. The STE's contribution is evaluated against these labels; learned artifact classification is deferred to future work (§9).

4.6 Evaluation

· Tension detection: precision, recall, F1 against expert labels.
· Context ablation: does structured context reduce false positives by ≥15% relative to context-free claim comparison? (Success criterion for H1.)
· Memory ablation: does persistent M_T improve non-redundancy of generated questions?
· Retrospective test: given \mathcal{K}_{t_0} before historical resolution, does \Pi(\mathfrak{t}^*) prioritize the historically productive tension?

