6. Evaluation Metrics

Tension Precision.

\mathrm{TP} = \frac{\#\{\text{validated tensions}\}}{\#\{\text{detected tensions}\}}.

Resolution Efficiency.

\mathrm{RE} = \frac{\Delta U}{\mathrm{Cost}(E)}.

Discrimination Efficiency.

\mathrm{DE} = \frac{\mathrm{VOI}(E)}{\mathrm{Cost}(E)}.

Discovery Yield.

\mathrm{DY} = \frac{\#\{\text{validated novel findings}\}}{\text{investigation budget}}.

Tension-to-Discovery Conversion.

\mathrm{TDC} = \frac{\#\{\text{tensions producing validated insight}\}}{\#\{\text{investigated tensions}\}}.

Definition of "validated novel finding." A finding is validated if (i) it is not entailed by \mathcal{K}_{t_0}, (ii) it survives expert review, and (iii) it is supported by an independent experimental or observational record. A finding is novel if it is not present in the retrieval corpus at time t_0 and is not a paraphrase of an existing claim. These criteria are pre-registered to prevent circularity.

Cost units. All costs are expressed in explicit units: API calls, compute hours, retrieval budget, expert review hours. Costs are normalized to a common scale for cross-baseline comparison.

TDC is the central metric. The system should not merely produce many contradictions; it should determine which contradictions are scientifically productive.

