7. Baselines, Ablations, and Controls

7.1 Baselines

ID Description
B0 Standard LLM research agent: retrieval + question answering.
B1 Hypothesis generator: goal → hypotheses → evaluation.
B2 Knowledge-graph scientist: KG → graph reasoning → hypotheses.
B3 Active-learning scientist: hypotheses → information-gain selection.
B4 Falsification scientist: hypothesis → adversarial test → revision.
B5 Tension Engine: knowledge → tensions → counterexamples → discriminating experiments.
B6 Integrated system: M_G + M_T + \mathcal{K}, with gaps and tensions jointly controlling exploration.

Resource parity. B0–B6 use the same LLM, the same retrieval corpus, and the same cost budget. Differences between baselines are therefore attributable to the control architecture, not to resource asymmetry.

7.2 Negative controls

· C1 — Random tensions: tensions selected uniformly at random from M_T.
· C2 — Plausibility-only: tensions ranked by LLM plausibility score.
· C3 — Novelty-only: tensions ranked by embedding novelty.
· C4 — Context-free contradiction: naive c_i \perp c_j detection without context, assumptions, or uncertainty.

7.3 Ablation design

A factorial design isolates the contribution of each component:

Factor Levels
Context representation on / off
Persistent memory M_T on / off
Tension prioritization on / off
VOI vs. linear surrogate VOI / surrogate

This yields 2^4 = 16 conditions. Ablations are run on TensionBench v0 and on the retrospective benchmark.

