8. Falsifiable Hypotheses and Success Criteria

H1 — Tension detection. A structured representation with context, assumptions, provenance, and uncertainty reduces false-positive contradiction detection compared with context-free claim comparison.

Criterion: ≥15% relative reduction in false positives on TensionBench v0.

H2 — Persistent memory. Systems with persistent M_T generate a higher proportion of non-redundant research questions than systems without persistent tension state.

Criterion: ≥20% relative reduction in question redundancy, measured by embedding similarity against prior questions.

H3 — Experimental discrimination. For competing scientific explanations, tension-driven experiment selection produces greater expected hypothesis discrimination per unit cost than random selection.

Criterion: ≥15% relative improvement in Discrimination Efficiency (DE) over B3 (active-learning).

H4 — Discovery efficiency. A scientific agent using \mathcal{K} + M_G + M_T achieves greater discovery efficiency than an otherwise equivalent agent using \mathcal{K} + M_G alone.

Criterion: ≥20% relative improvement on Discovery Yield in the retrospective benchmark.

H5 — Boundary discovery. Tension-driven search identifies boundary conditions more efficiently than pure hypothesis-generation systems in domains with context-dependent claims.

Criterion: ≥20% relative improvement in the number of correctly identified boundary conditions per unit cost, measured against expert-labeled boundaries.

Central falsification criterion. If tension-driven selection does not outperform active-learning, novelty, and plausibility baselines on TDC and Discovery Yield in the retrospective benchmark, the central hypothesis must be rejected or substantially revised.

---

9. Safety and Epistemic Controls

A contradiction engine has an important failure mode: it may learn to manufacture contradictions because contradictions are rewarded.

\boxed{\text{Contradiction generation} \;\neq\; \text{Scientific discovery}}

The architecture distinguishes:

\text{true contradiction} \quad\text{vs.}\quad \text{artifact},


\text{interesting uncertainty} \quad\text{vs.}\quad \text{mere disagreement in wording}.

Every tension carries provenance and evidence. The system never silently converts

"Study A reports X; Study B reports Y"

into

"Science has established a contradiction."

Instead the pipeline is

\text{Tension} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Context analysis} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Evidence audit} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Classification} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Action}.

Explicit limitation. During Phases I–III, artifact-vs-genuine-tension classification relies on human expert labeling, not on a learned classifier. The roadmap does not assume that the hard epistemic problem is solved by the architecture; it assumes only that the benchmark is annotated by experts, and that the STE's contribution can be evaluated against those annotations. Learned classification of artifacts is deferred to future work.

Metric gaming. TDC can be gamed by generating many trivial tensions that later "resolve" into known facts. The following countermeasures are adopted:

· Pre-registration of the validation criteria for "validated insight" (§6);
· Provenance audits for every tension;
· Penalties for low-provenance contradictions in the priority function (§3.11);
· Adversarial critic agents that attempt to dissolve reported tensions;
· Human expert validation for high-stakes domains.

Additional controls: reproducibility checks; reward penalties for low-provenance contradictions; explicit reporting of false-positive and artifact rates.

