The novelty hypothesis is:

A general-purpose autonomous scientific architecture in which persistent, provenance-aware tensions form an explicit computational control state governing question generation, counterexample construction, experiment selection, and knowledge revision.

Before claiming novelty, Phase I conducts a systematic review covering:

· truth maintenance systems (de Kleer, 1986);
· AGM belief revision (Alchourrón et al., 1985; Gärdenfors, 1988; Hansson, 1999);
· argumentation frameworks (Dung, 1995);
