13. Retrospective Evaluation Using Swanson ABC Logic

Swanson's ABC model formalizes literature-based discovery: given two non-interacting literatures L_1 linking A to B and L_2 linking B to C, a hypothesis A \to C may be discoverable by bridging. Swanson's classic examples are Raynaud's disease + fish oil (1986) and migraine + magnesium (1988).

The STE's retrospective evaluation uses the same logic:

1. Fix the knowledge state \mathcal{K}_{t_0} to literature before the historical resolution.
2. Apply the STE tension detector to \mathcal{K}_{t_0}.
3. Compute \Pi(\mathfrak{t}) for each detected tension.
4. Ask: does \Pi(\mathfrak{t}^*) rank the historically productive tension highly?
5. Compare against baselines B0–B4 and negative controls C1–C4.

If the STE's top-ranked tensions correlate with historically productive tensions significantly better than baselines, the central hypothesis is supported. If not, it is falsified.

Hindsight bias controls.

· Temporal cutoff: the retrieval corpus is restricted to documents published before the resolution date.
· Model contamination: models with known training cutoffs are used; post-cutoff literature is masked.
· Leakage checks: embeddings of pre-cutoff claims are compared against post-cutoff claims to detect implicit leakage.
· Held-out resolutions: a subset of resolutions is withheld from the evaluation pipeline until final scoring.
· Adversarial critic: an independent critic agent attempts to identify hindsight leakage in the STE's outputs.

---

14. Limitations

The deepest limitation is epistemological. Scientific contradictions are often only apparent. A system may interpret

P(x \mid \Gamma_1) \quad\text{and}\quad \neg P(x \mid \Gamma_2)

as contradictory when \Gamma_1 \neq \Gamma_2. The framework therefore depends heavily on context reconstruction. This is not a minor engineering issue; it may be the central scientific challenge of the project.

The RevisionCost formulation (§3.5) makes this challenge computable: if the minimal context edit required to remove the tension is small, the tension is likely contextual; if it is large, the tension is likely genuine. The threshold \theta is the operational encoding of this distinction.

Additional limitations.

· VOI assumes a bounded hypothesis space. Open-ended science does not have a pre-enumerable \mathcal{H}. The STE operates with bounded hypothesis sets and treats the boundary as a domain-specific design choice; extensions to Bayesian nonparametrics or structure-learning are left to future work.
· AGM idealization. The framework assumes deductive closure and logical omniscience at the level of the revision-cost functional. Resource-bounded and paraconsistent extensions are not implemented.
· LLM dependence. Claim extraction, context extraction, and question generation depend on LLM capabilities. Errors in extraction propagate to tension detection and prioritization.
· Single-domain v0. TensionBench v0 is built in one domain. Cross-domain generalization is planned (Phase X) but not demonstrated.
· Annotation cost. Expert annotation of 600 tension pairs is expensive. Quality is prioritized over quantity; the fallback is 300 high-quality pairs.
· TDC gaming. The metric is vulnerable to trivial-tension strategies; countermeasures are specified (§9) but not proven sufficient.

---

15. Conclusion

Scientific knowledge grows not only because researchers discover new facts, but because existing explanations fail. A scientific anomaly can expose a missing variable. A contradiction can expose a missing context. A disagreement can expose a boundary condition. Two apparently incompatible theories can reveal an unrecognized intermediate mechanism.

The Scientific Tension Engine turns these phenomena into explicit computational objects.

Its central hypothesis is:

\boxed{\text{Do not only ask AI what is known.}}

\boxed{\text{Ask AI where its knowledge does not fit together.}}

\boxed{\text{Make that tension experimentally actionable.}}

The next scientifically important step is not more architecture. It is to build TensionBench v0 and attempt to falsify the central hypothesis:

\boxed{\text{Does tension-driven scientific exploration discover more useful knowledge per unit investigation than existing strategies?}}

If no, the architecture is rejected or revised. If yes, the contribution is substantially more interesting than another autonomous research agent: a scientific control architecture that governs why an autonomous scientist investigates one uncertainty rather than another.

