A.3 Standing assumptions

The following are adopted throughout unless explicitly relaxed.

Assumption A.1 (Finite claims). At any time t, C_t is finite.

Assumption A.2 (Decidable context and assumptions). For every claim c_i, the context \Gamma_i and assumption set A_i are finite sets of literals over a decidable fragment of \mathcal{L}.

Assumption A.3 (Credal uncertainty). Each U_i is a nonempty convex set of probability distributions over a common measurable space, with a distinguished reference element p_i \in U_i.

Assumption A.4 (Bounded hypothesis space). The hypothesis set \mathcal{H} used in the VOI functional is finite or a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^d.

Idealization A.5 (AGM idealization). The revision-cost functional is defined relative to an AGM belief set, i.e., a deductively closed set. Resource-bounded and paraconsistent extensions are deferred to future work.

Idealization A.6 (Independence of provenance and content). Provenance quality q(P) is evaluated independently of the semantic content of the associated claim. This is a simplification; provenance-content interactions are a domain-specific extension.

