Appendix B — AGM Belief Revision: Postulates, Representation, and Results

This appendix collects the AGM machinery used in §3.4 and §3.5 of the main text. All results here are standard; references are given.

B.1 Belief sets and expansion

Definition B.1 (Belief set). A belief set is a set K \subseteq \mathcal{L} such that K = \mathrm{Cn}(K). The absurd belief set is K_\bot = \mathcal{L}.

Definition B.2 (Expansion). For \alpha \in \mathcal{L}, the expansion of K by \alpha is
K + \alpha \;=\; \mathrm{Cn}(K \cup \{\alpha\}).

Proposition B.3 (AGM expansion postulates, standard). Expansion satisfies:

· (K+1) K + \alpha is a belief set;
· (K+2) \alpha \in K + \alpha;
· (K+3) K \subseteq K + \alpha;
· (K+4) If \alpha \in K, then K + \alpha = K;
· (K+5) If K \subseteq H, then K + \alpha \subseteq H + \alpha;
· (K+6) K + \alpha is the smallest belief set satisfying (K+1)–(K+5).

Reference: Alchourrón, Gärdenfors & Makinson (1985); Hansson (1999, Ch. 3).

B.2 Contraction

Definition B.4 (Remainder set). For K a belief set and \alpha \in \mathcal{L},
K \perp \alpha \;=\; \{\, X \subseteq K \;:\; X \not\vdash \alpha \;\text{ and }\; X \subsetneq Y \subseteq K \Rightarrow Y \vdash \alpha \,\}.


Elements of K \perp \alpha are the maximal subsets of K that do not entail \alpha.

Definition B.5 (Partial-meet contraction). A selection function for K is a function \gamma that assigns to every nonempty K \perp \alpha a nonempty subset \gamma(K \perp \alpha) \subseteq K \perp \alpha, and to \varnothing the value \{K\}. The partial-meet contraction of K by \alpha is
K - \alpha \;=\; \bigcap \gamma(K \perp \alpha).

Definition B.6 (Epistemic entrenchment). An epistemic entrenchment for K is a binary relation \leq_E on \mathcal{L} satisfying:

· (EE1) If \alpha \leq_E \beta and \beta \leq_E \gamma, then \alpha \leq_E \gamma (transitivity);
· (EE2) If \alpha \vdash \beta, then \alpha \leq_E \beta;
· (EE3) For all \alpha, \beta: \alpha \leq_E \alpha \wedge \beta or \beta \leq_E \alpha \wedge \beta;
· (EE4) When K \neq K_\bot: \alpha \notin K - \alpha iff \alpha \leq_E \beta for all \beta \in K;
· (EE5) If \beta \leq_E \alpha for all \beta, then \vdash \alpha.

Proposition B.7 (AGM contraction postulates, standard). Partial-meet contraction satisfies:

· (K−1) K - \alpha is a belief set;
· (K−2) K - \alpha \subseteq K;
· (K−3) If \alpha \notin K, then K - \alpha = K;
· (K−4) If \nvdash \alpha, then \alpha \notin K - \alpha;
· (K−5) If \alpha \in K, then K \subseteq (K - \alpha) + \alpha;
· (K−6) If \vdash \alpha \leftrightarrow \beta, then K - \alpha = K - \beta;
· (K−7) (K - \alpha) \cap (K - \beta) \subseteq K - (\alpha \wedge \beta);
· (K−8) If \alpha \notin K - (\alpha \wedge \beta), then K - (\alpha \wedge \beta) \subseteq K - \alpha.

Reference: Alchourrón, Gärdenfors & Makinson (1985, Thm. 2.3); Hansson (1999, Thm. 4.7).

B.3 Revision

Definition B.8 (Levi identity). Revision is defined from contraction and expansion by
K * \alpha \;=\; (K - \neg\alpha) + \alpha.

Definition B.9 (Harper identity). Contraction is recoverable from revision by
K - \alpha \;=\; K \cap (K * \neg\alpha).

Proposition B.10 (Equivalence, standard). The Levi and Harper identities are equivalent under the AGM postulates. Revision defined via Levi satisfies:

· (K*1) K * \alpha is a belief set;
· (K*2) \alpha \in K * \alpha;
· (K*3) K * \alpha \subseteq K + \alpha;
· (K*4) If \neg\alpha \notin K, then K + \alpha \subseteq K * \alpha;
· (K*5) K * \alpha = K_\bot iff \vdash \neg\alpha;
· (K*6) If \vdash \alpha \leftrightarrow \beta, then K * \alpha = K * \beta;
· (K*7) K * (\alpha \wedge \beta) \subseteq (K * \alpha) + \beta;
· (K*8) If \neg\beta \notin K * \alpha, then (K * \alpha) + \beta \subseteq K * (\alpha \wedge \beta).

Reference: Gärdenfors (1988); Hansson (1999, Ch. 3).

B.4 Grove's sphere semantics

Definition B.11 (System of spheres). A system of spheres centered on K is a collection \mathbb{S} of subsets of M_{\mathcal{L}} such that:

· (S1) \mathbb{S} is totally ordered by \subseteq;
· (S2) \bigcap \mathbb{S} = K \cap M_{\mathcal{L}} (the innermost sphere is the set of maximal consistent supersets of K);
· (S3) M_{\mathcal{L}} \in \mathbb{S};
· (S4) For any \alpha \in \mathcal{L}, if some sphere intersects [\alpha] = \{M \in M_{\mathcal{L}} : \alpha \in M\}, there is a smallest such sphere.

Proposition B.12 (Grove representation, standard). Every AGM revision operator * is representable by a system of spheres via
K * \alpha \;=\; \bigcap \mathbb{S}_\alpha, \qquad \mathbb{S}_\alpha = \text{smallest sphere intersecting } [\alpha],


and conversely. Reference: Grove (1988).

Corollary B.13. The revision-cost functional of §3.5 measures, informally, the "distance" from the outermost sphere compatible with c_i to the innermost sphere containing a model of c_i \wedge c_j.

B.5 Relational and transitive partial-meet contraction

Definition B.14 (Relational partial meet). A partial-meet contraction -\gamma is relational if there is a relation \preceq over subsets of K such that
\gamma(K \perp \alpha) = \{\, X \in K \perp \alpha \;:\; Y \preceq X \text{ for all } Y \in K \perp \alpha \,\}.


It is transitively relational if \preceq can be taken transitive.

Proposition B.15 (Standard). A contraction operation satisfies (K−1)–(K−8) if and only if it is transitively relational partial meet. Reference: Alchourrón, Gärdenfors & Makinson (1985, Thm. 3.3).

B.6 Explicit limits of the AGM framework

The following limitations are acknowledged in the main text (§3.4, §14) and are not fixed by any result above.

1. Deductive closure. AGM belief sets are deductively closed. Real scientific corpora are not.
2. Logical omniscience. The agent is assumed to know all consequences of its beliefs.
3. Consistency requirement. AGM does not operate on inconsistent belief sets; paraconsistent or non-prioritized revision is required for the full scientific case.
4. No uncertainty. AGM treats beliefs as categorical. Probabilistic belief revision (e.g., Jeffrey conditioning, Adams conditioning) is a separate framework.
5. No resource bounds. Contraction is assumed to be computable exactly. In practice, the RevisionCost is NP-hard (App. C.6).

These limits motivate the idealizational reading adopted in the main text: AGM is used as a normative reference for the revision-cost functional, not as a descriptive account of scientific belief.

