Appendix C — RevisionCost: Formal Properties, Closed Forms, and Complexity

This appendix develops §3.5 of the main text.

C.1 Setup

Fix two claims
c_i = (s_i, p_i, o_i, \Gamma_i, A_i, U_i, P_i, E_i), \qquad
c_j = (s_j, p_j, o_j, \Gamma_j, A_j, U_j, P_j, E_j).

Let
\mathcal{B}_{ij} = \mathrm{Cn}(\Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j \cup A_i \cup A_j \cup U_i \cup U_j)


be the joint belief set. Here \Gamma_i, \Gamma_j are treated as finite sets of literals; A_i, A_j as finite sets of literals; U_i, U_j as credal sets with distinguished reference elements p_i, p_j.

Definition C.1 (Admissibility). A triple (\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*) is admissible for (c_i, c_j) if:

1. \Gamma^* \subseteq \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j and \Gamma^* is consistent;
2. A^* \subseteq A_i \cup A_j and A^* is consistent;
3. U^* is a probability distribution on the common space of U_i, U_j;
4. c_i and c_j are both admissible under (\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*), i.e., neither is ruled out by the revised context, assumptions, or uncertainty.

Definition C.2 (Consistency set). The consistency set is
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} = \{\, U \;:\; (\Gamma^*, A^*, U) \text{ admissible for some } \Gamma^* \subseteq \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j,\; A^* \subseteq A_i \cup A_j \,\}.

Definition C.3 (RevisionCost, formal). With \lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3, \lambda_4 > 0,
\rho(c_i, c_j) \;=\; \inf_{(\Gamma^*, A^*, U^*) \text{ admissible}} \Bigl[
\lambda_1 d_\Gamma(\Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j, \Gamma^*) + \lambda_2 d_A(A_i \cup A_j, A^*) + \lambda_3 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U^* \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}}) + \lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij}))
\Bigr].

We adopt d_\Gamma = d_A = |\cdot \Delta \cdot|, the cardinality of the symmetric difference. Let P_{ij} = P_i \cup P_j and q(P_{ij}) = \min(q(P_i), q(P_j)). The reference U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} is defined in C.4.

C.2 Existence and well-definedness

Proposition C.4 (Well-definedness). If \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} \neq \varnothing, the infimum in Definition C.3 is attained, and \rho(c_i, c_j) \in [0, \infty).

Proof. The context and assumption spaces are finite by Assumption A.2, so there are finitely many pairs (\Gamma^*, A^*). For each pair, either no U makes the triple admissible (in which case the term is excluded), or the admissible U-set is nonempty. The map U \mapsto D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}}) is lower semi-continuous and nonnegative on the space of probability distributions with the topology of weak convergence. On a compact set of distributions (which \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} is, when nonempty and closed), the infimum is attained. Since the space of pairs (\Gamma^*, A^*) is finite, the outer infimum is attained at some admissible triple. Nonnegativity of each term gives \rho \geq 0. \square

Corollary C.5 (Trivial consistency). If \mathcal{B}_{ij} is consistent (i.e., c_i, c_j are jointly consistent under \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j and A_i \cup A_j), then taking \Gamma^* = \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j, A^* = A_i \cup A_j, U^* = U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} yields \rho = \lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij})), which is zero if and only if q(P_{ij}) = 1.

Remark C.6. The trivial case shows that the provenance penalty is a baseline cost, not a contradiction-specific one. The detection threshold \theta should therefore be calibrated so that \tau_{ij} \geq \theta requires \rho to exceed \lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij})), i.e., that some context, assumption, or uncertainty revision is required.

Corollary C.7 (Boundedness). For all c_i, c_j, if \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} \neq \varnothing:
0 \;\leq\; \rho(c_i, c_j) \;\leq\; \lambda_1 |\Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j| + \lambda_2 |A_i \cup A_j| + \lambda_3 D_{\max} + \lambda_4,


where D_{\max} = \sup_{U} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}}) over the admissible U-set. If \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} = \varnothing, we define \rho = +\infty by convention.

C.3 Monotonicity and invariance

Proposition C.8 (Monotonicity in incompatibility). Suppose c_i, c_j are two claim pairs with c_i', c_j' obtained from c_i, c_j by removing a context literal from \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j or an assumption from A_i \cup A_j. Then
\rho(c_i', c_j') \;\leq\; \rho(c_i, c_j) \quad \text{whenever both are finite.}

Proof. Removing a literal enlarges the admissible set of (\Gamma^*, A^*) pairs, since any admissible revision for (c_i, c_j) remains admissible for (c_i', c_j'). The infimum over a larger set is no greater. \square

Proposition C.9 (Invariance under relabeling). If \sigma: \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{L} is a bijective renaming of atoms that preserves the logical structure and the sets \Gamma_i, A_i, P_i, E_i up to \sigma, then
\rho(\sigma(c_i), \sigma(c_j)) = \rho(c_i, c_j).

Proof. Immediate: all set operations and KL divergences are invariant under measure-preserving relabeling of the underlying space. \square

Proposition C.10 (Symmetry). \rho(c_i, c_j) = \rho(c_j, c_i).

Proof. Every term in the objective is symmetric in i \leftrightarrow j. \square

C.4 Well-definedness of the KL term

Definition C.11 (Reference joint uncertainty). Let U_i, U_j be credal sets with reference elements p_i \in U_i, p_j \in U_j, each a probability measure on a common measurable space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}). Define
U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} \;=\; \text{maximum-entropy coupling of } p_i, p_j \text{ under a fixed dependence structure.}


By default we take the product coupling U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} = p_i \otimes p_j; alternatives include Gaussian copula couplings with specified \rho.

Proposition C.12 (Existence and uniqueness of the KL projection). Suppose \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} is nonempty, closed, and convex in the topology of weak convergence. Then
U^* = \arg\min_{U \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}}} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}})


exists and is unique.

Proof. D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\cdot \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}}) is strictly convex and lower semi-continuous on the space of probability measures with finite first moment. On a nonempty closed convex subset of a locally convex space, a strictly convex lower semi-continuous functional attains a unique minimum (standard result in convex analysis; cf. Csiszár 1975, "I-divergence geometry of probability distributions and minimization problems," Annals of Probability 3(1): 146–158). \square

C.5 Closed forms

C.5.1 Boolean case (degenerate uncertainty)

Proposition C.13 (Boolean RevisionCost). Suppose U_i, U_j are Dirac measures (so \lambda_3 = 0), and \Gamma_i, A_i are finite sets of literals. Then
\rho(c_i, c_j) = \lambda_1 \cdot \min_{\Gamma^*} |(\Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j) \Delta \Gamma^*| + \lambda_2 \cdot \min_{A^*} |(A_i \cup A_j) \Delta A^*| + \lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij})),


where the minima range over \Gamma^* \subseteq \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j and A^* \subseteq A_i \cup A_j such that the corresponding revision restores joint consistency of c_i, c_j. Equivalently,
\rho(c_i, c_j) = \lambda_1 \cdot \mathrm{MCS}_\Gamma(c_i, c_j) + \lambda_2 \cdot \mathrm{MCS}_A(c_i, c_j) + \lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij})),


where \mathrm{MCS}_\Gamma and \mathrm{MCS}_A are the minimum correction set sizes for the context and assumption components respectively.

Proof. With degenerate uncertainty, the admissible triples reduce to pairs (\Gamma^*, A^*). For each such pair, admissibility means \Gamma^* \cup A^* is consistent with both claims. The objective is the weighted sum of two cardinality distances plus a constant. The minimum over finitely many admissible pairs is attained. \square

C.5.2 Gaussian case (mean-constrained consistency)

Proposition C.14 (KL projection onto linear mean constraints). Let U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}} = \mathcal{N}(\mu_0, \Sigma_0) on \mathbb{R}^d with \Sigma_0 \succ 0. Let the consistency set be
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{cons}} = \{\, \mathcal{N}(\mu, \Sigma) \;:\; A \mu = b,\; \Sigma \succ 0 \,\},


for given A \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times d}, b \in \mathbb{R}^k. Then
U^* = \mathcal{N}(\mu^*, \Sigma_0), \qquad \mu^* = \mu_0 + \Sigma_0 A^\top (A \Sigma_0 A^\top)^{-1} (b - A \mu_0),


and
D_{\mathrm{KL}}(U^* \| U_{ij}^{\mathrm{ref}}) = \tfrac{1}{2} (b - A \mu_0)^\top (A \Sigma_0 A^\top)^{-1} (b - A \mu_0).

Proof. Write D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathcal{N}(\mu, \Sigma) \| \mathcal{N}(\mu_0, \Sigma_0)) using the standard formula
D_{\mathrm{KL}} = \tfrac{1}{2} \bigl[ \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma_0^{-1} \Sigma) + (\mu_0 - \mu)^\top \Sigma_0^{-1} (\mu_0 - \mu) - d + \ln \tfrac{\det \Sigma_0}{\det \Sigma} \bigr].


For fixed \mu, the \Sigma-dependent part is \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma_0^{-1} \Sigma) - \ln \det \Sigma + \mathrm{const}, which is minimized at \Sigma = \Sigma_0 (the gradient \Sigma_0^{-1} - \Sigma^{-1} vanishes there; the second derivative is \Sigma^{-2} \succ 0). With \Sigma = \Sigma_0 fixed, minimize f(\mu) = (\mu_0 - \mu)^\top \Sigma_0^{-1} (\mu_0 - \mu) subject to A\mu = b. The Lagrangian is
L(\mu, \nu) = (\mu_0 - \mu)^\top \Sigma_0^{-1} (\mu_0 - \mu) + \nu^\top (A\mu - b).


Stationarity in \mu: -2 \Sigma_0^{-1}(\mu_0 - \mu) + A^\top \nu = 0, i.e., \mu = \mu_0 + \tfrac{1}{2} \Sigma_0 A^\top \nu. Substituting A\mu = b: A \mu_0 + \tfrac{1}{2} A \Sigma_0 A^\top \nu = b, so \nu = 2 (A \Sigma_0 A^\top)^{-1}(b - A\mu_0). Substituting back gives \mu^*. The value of the objective at \mu^* is f(\mu^*) = \tfrac{1}{2}(b - A\mu_0)^\top (A \Sigma_0 A^\top)^{-1} (b - A\mu_0), and the KL divergence equals \tfrac{1}{2} f(\mu^*) because the covariance terms cancel (since \Sigma^* = \Sigma_0): \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma_0^{-1} \Sigma_0) - d + \ln(\det \Sigma_0 / \det \Sigma_0) = 0. \square

Remark C.15. When the consistency set also constrains the covariance, the joint (\mu, \Sigma)-projection is not in closed form in general and requires numerical optimization. The standard case (constraints on the mean only) admits the closed form above. This matches the main-text caveat.

C.6 Complexity

Proposition C.16 (Boolean RevisionCost is NP-hard). Computing \rho(c_i, c_j) in the Boolean case (Definition C.13) is NP-hard under polynomial-time many-one reductions.

Proof. Reduce from minimum vertex cover (MVC). Let G = (V, E) with |V| = n. Construct a propositional language with atoms \{x_v : v \in V\} \cup \{z\}. Let the joint claim pair be encoded so that the following constraint set \mathcal{S}_G is the joint assumption set:
\mathcal{S}_G = \{\neg x_v : v \in V\} \cup \{x_u \vee x_v : (u,v) \in E\}.


Assign weights w(\neg x_v) = 1 for each v, w(x_u \vee x_v) = M for each edge, where M > n. The minimum correction set of \mathcal{S}_G is the minimum-weight subset whose removal makes \mathcal{S}_G satisfiable.

Claim: the minimum correction set has weight equal to \tau(G) + \varepsilon, where \tau(G) is the MVC size of G and \varepsilon corrects for the possibility of removing edge clauses.

Take any assignment \alpha of x_v. The clause \neg x_v is violated iff x_v = 1. The clause x_u \vee x_v is violated iff both x_u = x_v = 0. Let S = \{v : x_v = 1\}. Then the set of violated clauses is
\{\neg x_v : v \notin S\} \cup \{x_u \vee x_v : (u,v) \in E,\, u \notin S,\, v \notin S\}.


Removing exactly the violated clauses makes \mathcal{S}_G satisfiable. The cost is |V \setminus S| + M \cdot |\{\text{edges disjoint from } S\}|.

The minimum over S of this cost: if S is a vertex cover, the edge term is 0, and cost = n - |S|, minimized at |S| = \tau(G), cost = n - \tau(G). If S is not a vertex cover, cost \geq n - |S| + M \geq M > n \geq n - \tau(G). Hence the minimum correction set weight is n - \tau(G) + \varepsilon', where \varepsilon' is 0. Therefore computing the minimum correction set size yields \tau(G), and MVC is NP-hard; hence so is the minimum correction set problem, and therefore \rho in the Boolean case. \square

Corollary C.17 (General RevisionCost is NP-hard). The general RevisionCost (Definition C.3) is NP-hard, since it contains the Boolean case as a special case (\lambda_3 = 0, degenerate uncertainty).

Remark C.18 (Relation to MUS enumeration). The Boolean RevisionCost is equivalent to finding a minimum-weight correction set, which is the minimum hitting set of the family of all minimal unsatisfiable subsets (MUSes) of \mathcal{S}_G. MUS enumeration is itself computationally hard (#P-hard for counting); finding a minimum hitting set without enumerating all MUSes remains NP-hard. The main-text phrasing "closely related to MUS enumeration" is therefore correct; the stronger claim "NP-hard via reduction to MUS enumeration" is also correct but should be read as "via reduction to the minimum correction set problem, which is dual to MUS hitting set."

References for standard results used: Liffiton & Sakallah (2008); Liffiton et al. (2016); Marques-Silva et al. (2013, "Minimal sets over monotone predicates in Boolean formulae," CAV).

C.7 Tension score properties

Proposition C.19. The tension score \tau_{ij} = 1 - e^{-\rho(c_i,c_j)/\rho_0} satisfies:

1. \tau_{ij} \in [0, 1) for \rho \in [0, \infty);
2. \tau_{ij} \to 0 as \rho \to 0^+;
3. \tau_{ij} \to 1 as \rho \to \infty;
4. \tau_{ij} is strictly increasing in \rho;
5. \tau_{ij}(\rho_0 \ln 2) = 1/2 (the median point).

Proof. Direct from the properties of the exponential. \square

Proposition C.20 (Threshold monotonicity). For fixed \theta, the predicate \tau_{ij} \geq \theta is equivalent to \rho(c_i,c_j) \geq \rho_0 \ln(1/(1-\theta)).

Proof. \tau_{ij} \geq \theta \iff e^{-\rho/\rho_0} \leq 1-\theta \iff -\rho/\rho_0 \leq \ln(1-\theta) \iff \rho \geq -\rho_0 \ln(1-\theta) = \rho_0 \ln(1/(1-\theta)). \square

Corollary C.21 (Calibration principle). Given a target sensitivity \theta and a known provenance baseline \rho_q = \lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij})), the scale \rho_0 should be chosen so that \rho_0 \ln(1/(1-\theta)) > \rho_q, i.e.,
\rho_0 > \frac{\lambda_4 (1 - q(P_{ij}))}{\ln(1/(1-\theta))}.

