Appendix D — ATMS Nogood Semantics: Formal Correspondence

This appendix develops §3.9 of the main text. We follow de Kleer (1986) and Forbus & de Kleer (1993).

D.1 Recap of the ATMS

Definition D.1 (ATMS). An Assumption-Based Truth Maintenance System consists of:

· a set of nodes (propositions);
· a set of assumptions \mathcal{A}, each a distinguished proposition;
· a set of justifications of the form (a_1, \ldots, a_k) \to n, where each a_i is a node or assumption and n is a node;
· for each node n, a label \mathcal{L}(n), a set of environments.

Definition D.2 (Environment). An environment is a subset e \subseteq \mathcal{A}. An environment is consistent if it is not a superset of any nogood.

Definition D.3 (Nogood). A nogood is a set e \subseteq \mathcal{A} such that e \cup \{\text{background}\} \vdash \bot. A nogood is minimal if no proper subset is a nogood.

Definition D.4 (Label). The label \mathcal{L}(n) of node n is the set of minimal consistent environments e such that e \cup \{\text{background}\} \vdash n.

Proposition D.5 (Label propagation, standard). Given a justification (a_1, \ldots, a_k) \to n, the label of n is updated by
\mathcal{L}(n) \;\leftarrow\; \mathcal{L}(n) \cup \{\, e_1 \cup \cdots \cup e_k \;:\; e_i \in \mathcal{L}(a_i),\; e_1 \cup \cdots \cup e_k \text{ consistent} \,\}.


Propagation is sound and complete for Horn justifications. Reference: de Kleer (1986, Thm. 2).

D.2 Correspondence to Tension Memory

Definition D.6 (Tagged assumption space). Let \mathcal{A} be the assumption space
\mathcal{A} = \Gamma \cup A \cup \{\text{background}\}


viewed as a set of literals. Define the tagged assumption space
\mathcal{A}' = (\Gamma \cup A) \times \{+, -\}


where + and - denote the supporting and conflicting claim respectively.

Definition D.7 (Tension environment). For a tension \mathfrak{t} = (C^+, C^-, \Gamma, A, E, P, U, S, H) with C^+ = \{c_i\}, C^- = \{c_j\}, the tension environment is
e_{\mathfrak{t}} \;=\; (\Gamma_i \cup A_i) \times \{+\} \;\cup\; (\Gamma_j \cup A_j) \times \{-\}.

Proposition D.8 (Correspondence). Under the identification \mathcal{A} \leftrightarrow \mathcal{A}', the tension record \mathfrak{t} is a nogood in the ATMS sense: e_{\mathfrak{t}} \cup \{\text{background}\} \vdash \bot, and e_{\mathfrak{t}} is minimal iff no proper subset of e_{\mathfrak{t}} (in either the + or - component) is a nogood.

Proof. By construction of \mathcal{B}_{ij} (§C.1), the joint belief set is inconsistent. Therefore there exists a minimal inconsistent subset e \subseteq \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j \cup A_i \cup A_j. Under the tagging identification, e corresponds to a subset of e_{\mathfrak{t}}. If e_{\mathfrak{t}} is minimal, then e = e_{\mathfrak{t}}, and e_{\mathfrak{t}} is the minimal nogood. If e_{\mathfrak{t}} is not minimal, then the minimal nogood is e, a proper subset. \square

Remark D.9. The tagging is necessary because \Gamma_i \cup \Gamma_j might be consistent on its own (the same literal cannot both be required and forbidden by \Gamma), yet the combination of claims could be inconsistent. Tagging separates "assumption required by c_i" from "assumption required by c_j", making the inconsistency detectable as a nogood.

Corollary D.10 (Augmented ATMS). The Tension Memory M_T is a nogood store in the sense of Definition D.3, augmented with:

· a status S tracking whether the nogood is active, contextualized, or retired;
· a history H recording the sequence of contractions attempted.

Proposition D.11 (Status transition preserves soundness). The transition function \delta_S (§3.9) preserves the ATMS invariant that every element of M_T is a nogood of \mathcal{A}'. In particular:

1. Transition unverified \to contextualized is admissible iff the revision small-cost outcome is recorded;
2. Transition unverified \to persistent is admissible iff the revision large-cost outcome is recorded;
3. Transition any \to rejected is admissible iff the provenance audit fails, in which case the nogood is removed from the active store but retained in H for audit.

Proof. (1)–(3) follow directly from the definitions of the outcomes in \Omega and the table in §3.9. Soundness follows from the fact that, in each transition, the underlying nogood e_{\mathfrak{t}} is not modified; only its status is updated. \square

D.3 Label propagation in M_T

Proposition D.12 (Efficient label propagation). If a new nogood e_{\mathfrak{t}} is added to M_T, the labels of affected nodes can be updated in time polynomial in |M_T| + |\mathcal{A}'|, provided the justifications are Horn.

Proof. The ATMS label propagation algorithm (de Kleer 1986, §4) propagates only through justifications whose antecedents have labels containing the newly invalidated environments. Each environment is invalidated at most once per nogood addition. The total work is O(|M_T| \cdot |\mathcal{A}'| \cdot \bar{k}), where \bar{k} is the maximum antecedent length. \square

Remark D.13 (Non-Horn case). For non-Horn justifications, label propagation is not polynomial in general; it inherits the complexity of the underlying logical fragment. The main text's TensionBench v0 restricts to Horn-definable claim structures to preserve tractability.

