Appendix E — Value of Information: Full Derivation and Linear Surrogate

This appendix develops §3.11 of the main text.

E.1 VOI as mutual information

Definition E.1 (VOI). Let H be a hypothesis variable, E an experiment, Y the outcome. The value of information of E with respect to H is
\mathrm{VOI}(E) \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim P(\cdot \mid E)} \Bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\bigl(P(H \mid Y, E) \,\big\|\, P(H)\bigr) \Bigr].

Proposition E.2 (Equivalence to mutual information). Under standard regularity conditions,
\mathrm{VOI}(E) \;=\; I(H; Y \mid E),


the mutual information between H and Y given E.

Proof.
I(H;Y \mid E) = \mathbb{E}_{H,Y \mid E}\!\left[ \ln \frac{P(H, Y \mid E)}{P(H) P(Y \mid E)} \right] = \mathbb{E}_{Y \mid E}\!\left[ \mathbb{E}_{H \mid Y, E}\!\left[ \ln \frac{P(H \mid Y, E)}{P(H)} \right] \right].


The inner expectation is exactly D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P(H \mid Y, E) \| P(H)). Taking the outer expectation gives \mathrm{VOI}(E). \square

Corollary E.3 (Non-negativity and zero condition). \mathrm{VOI}(E) \geq 0, with equality iff H \perp Y \mid E.

E.2 Convexity and optimal design

Proposition E.4 (Convexity). If the likelihood family \{P(Y \mid H, E) : E \in A_t\} is exponential with natural parameter linear in E, then \mathrm{VOI}(E) is concave in E.

Proof. Under the exponential-family assumption, I(H; Y \mid E) equals the expected log-normalizer difference, which is concave in the natural parameter (Cover & Thomas 2006, Thm. 2.7.4 for the Gaussian case; Brown 1986 for general exponential families). \square

E.3 Linear surrogate: rigorous statement and proof

Proposition E.5 (Linear VOI surrogate). Let H \in \mathbb{R}^d. Suppose:

1. The prior P(H) is Gaussian \mathcal{N}(\mu_0, \Sigma_0) with \Sigma_0 \succ 0.
2. The likelihood is Gaussian P(Y \mid H, E) = \mathcal{N}(X_E H, \Sigma_\varepsilon), with X_E \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times d} the design matrix and \Sigma_\varepsilon \succ 0.
3. The quantity I(\mathfrak{t}) = \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma_0 X_E^\top \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} X_E) is small (weak-signal regime).

Then
\mathrm{VOI}(E) \;=\; \tfrac{1}{2} \ln \det(I + \Sigma_0 X_E^\top \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} X_E) \;\approx\; \tfrac{1}{2} I(\mathfrak{t}) \;+\; O(I(\mathfrak{t})^2).

Proof. The posterior covariance is \Sigma_{\mathrm{post}} = (\Sigma_0^{-1} + X_E^\top \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} X_E)^{-1}. The mutual information for Gaussian H, Y is
I(H; Y) = \tfrac{1}{2} \ln \det(I + \Sigma_0 X_E^\top \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} X_E),


a standard result (Cover & Thomas 2006, Ch. 9.4). Expanding \ln \det(I + A) = \mathrm{tr}(A) - \tfrac{1}{2}\mathrm{tr}(A^2) + \cdots, we obtain the stated approximation. \square

Corollary E.6 (Admissibility of the weighted sum). In the weak-signal regime, the first-order approximation of VOI is linear in the Fisher information contributed by E. If the design matrix decomposes as X_E^\top \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} X_E = \sum_j X_j^\top \Sigma_{\varepsilon,j}^{-1} X_j (i.e., the experiment factorizes into J independent sub-experiments), then
\mathrm{VOI}(E) \;\approx\; \tfrac{1}{2} \sum_j \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma_0 X_j^\top \Sigma_{\varepsilon,j}^{-1} X_j),


which justifies the weighted-sum form \mathrm{VOI} \approx \sum_\bullet w_\bullet \cdot (\text{component}_\bullet).

Remark E.7 (Non-freeness of weights). The weights w_\bullet are not free: under the quadratic approximation, w_j = \tfrac{1}{2} \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma_0 X_j^\top \Sigma_{\varepsilon,j}^{-1} X_j) / \|\text{component}_j\|, i.e., they are determined by the local curvature and the normalization of each component. Free weights without this calibration risk collinearity between components (e.g., between discrimination D and falsification F) and are therefore inadmissible.

E.4 Additivity: what must not be double-counted

Proposition E.8 (Discrimination is a special case of VOI). If \mathcal{H} = \{M_1, \ldots, M_n\} is a discrete hypothesis set representing competing mechanisms, then model discrimination is measured by the mutual information I(\mathcal{H}; Y \mid E). Hence any term D(E) defined as "expected discrimination" is identical to \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathcal{H}) and must not be added to it.

Proof. Discrimination between mechanisms M_1, \ldots, M_n is the reduction in uncertainty about \mathcal{H}. The reduction in uncertainty is exactly the mutual information I(\mathcal{H}; Y \mid E), which by Proposition E.2 equals \mathrm{VOI}(E) with H = \mathcal{H}. \square

Corollary E.9 (Correct joint objective). The joint acquisition function is
\Pi(\mathfrak{t}) = \max_{E \in A_t} \bigl[\, \mathrm{VOI}(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) + \mu_F F(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) - \mu_C \mathrm{Cost}(E) \,\bigr],


where F is the falsification utility defined as the probability of producing an anomaly under the current best model. F is retained because it targets anomaly probability rather than entropy reduction; it is not an alternative to VOI.

E.5 Falsification utility

Definition E.10 (Falsification utility). Given a current best model M \in \mathcal{M}_t and an anomalous observation region \mathcal{Y}_{\mathrm{anom}}(M) \subseteq \mathcal{Y},
F(E \mid \mathfrak{t}) = P_{Y \sim P(\cdot \mid E)}\bigl(Y \in \mathcal{Y}_{\mathrm{anom}}(M)\bigr).

Proposition E.11 (Monotonicity). If \mathcal{Y}_{\mathrm{anom}}(M) \subseteq \mathcal{Y}_{\mathrm{anom}}(M'), then F(E \mid M) \leq F(E \mid M') for all E.

Proof. Direct from the definition. \square

Remark E.12 (Calibration of \mu_F). Setting \mu_F = 0 recovers the pure VOI objective. Setting \mu_F \to \infty recovers pure falsificationism. The intermediate regime weights anomaly probability against information gain. Calibration is domain-specific and is performed on TensionBench v0.

