Appendix J — Open Formal Problems

The following problems are not resolved in this proposal and are identified as targets for future work.

1. Revision of inconsistent belief sets. The AGM framework assumes consistency. A principled revision operator for inconsistent scientific corpora — possibly grounded in paraconsistent or non-prioritized revision — is not developed here.
2. Uncertainty of context extraction. \Gamma_i is treated as given. In practice, context must be extracted from text with error. A probabilistic treatment of context uncertainty is deferred.
3. Interaction between provenance and content. Assumption A.6 assumes independence. A joint model q(P \mid c) or q(P, c) is left open.
4. Tension score calibration. The mapping from \rho to \tau via 1 - e^{-\rho/\rho_0} is a convenient choice but not derived. Alternatives (logistic, piecewise) are admissible; the choice should be justified empirically.
5. Multi-claim tensions. The formalism treats tensions as pairwise. Tensions involving three or more claims are not yet characterized, though they can be encoded as higher-arity nogoods in the ATMS.
6. Dynamic hypothesis spaces. The VOI functional assumes a fixed \mathcal{H}. Extension to structure-learning and nonparametric hypothesis spaces is left open.
7. Computational complexity of the full control loop. While individual components (RevisionCost, VOI) have known complexity, the joint complexity of the loop \mathcal{C}: \mathcal{K}_t \to Q_t has not been characterized.
8. Guarantees on discovery. No theorem in this proposal asserts that the STE will discover productive tensions. The empirical hypotheses H1–H5 are the operational substitute for such guarantees.

